  <!-- ==================== QUICK DEFINITIONS ==================== -->
  <section id="definitions">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Quick Definitions</h2>
      <p>High-frequency definitions written in clear board-exam language. Memorise these exact wordings.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="def-grid">

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Chemical Reaction</h3>
        <p>A process in which two or more substances (reactants) react to form new substances (products) with completely different properties.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Chemical Equation</h3>
        <p>A symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using symbols and formulae of the reactants and products.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Balanced Chemical Equation</h3>
        <p>A chemical equation in which the number of atoms of each element is equal on both the reactant and product sides. It follows the law of conservation of mass.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Combination Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which two or more substances combine to form a single new product.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Decomposition Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which a single compound breaks down into two or more simpler substances.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Thermal Decomposition</h3>
        <p>A decomposition reaction that is carried out by heating the reactant.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Electrolytic Decomposition</h3>
        <p>A decomposition reaction that takes place when electric current is passed through the compound (usually in molten or aqueous state).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Photolytic Decomposition / Photochemical Decomposition</h3>
        <p>A decomposition reaction that is carried out by the action of light (usually sunlight).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Displacement Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which a more reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its compound.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Double Displacement Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which two compounds exchange their ions to form two new compounds.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Precipitation Reaction</h3>
        <p>A double displacement reaction in which one of the products is an insoluble solid (precipitate) that settles down.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Neutralisation Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which an acid reacts with a base to form salt and water. It is a special case of double displacement reaction.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Oxidation</h3>
        <p>In Class 10, oxidation is defined as the addition of oxygen to a substance or the removal of hydrogen from a substance.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Reduction</h3>
        <p>In Class 10, reduction is defined as the removal of oxygen from a substance or the addition of hydrogen to a substance.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Redox Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which oxidation and reduction take place simultaneously.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Corrosion</h3>
        <p>The process of slow conversion of metals into their undesirable compounds (oxides, carbonates, sulphides, etc.) by the action of air, moisture and chemicals present in the atmosphere.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Rancidity</h3>
        <p>The process of oxidation of oils and fats which results in an unpleasant smell and taste.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Acid</h3>
        <p>A substance which releases H⁺ ions (or H₃O⁺ ions) when dissolved in water. Acids turn blue litmus red.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Base</h3>
        <p>A substance which releases OH⁻ ions when dissolved in water. Bases turn red litmus blue and feel soapy to touch.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Salt</h3>
        <p>A compound formed when the hydrogen ion of an acid is replaced by a metal ion or ammonium ion.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Indicator</h3>
        <p>A substance that shows different colours in acidic and basic media and is used to test whether a substance is acidic or basic.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>pH Scale</h3>
        <p>A scale that measures the concentration of hydrogen ions in a solution. It ranges from 0 to 14. pH &lt; 7 is acidic, pH = 7 is neutral, pH &gt; 7 is basic.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Olfactory Indicators</h3>
        <p>Substances whose smell changes in acidic or basic medium (e.g., onion, vanilla, clove oil).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Chlor-Alkali Process</h3>
        <p>The process of electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride (brine) to produce sodium hydroxide, chlorine and hydrogen.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Bleaching Powder</h3>
        <p>Calcium oxychloride (CaOCl₂) prepared by the action of chlorine on dry slaked lime. Used for bleaching and disinfecting water.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Baking Soda</h3>
        <p>Sodium hydrogen carbonate (NaHCO₃). Used in baking, as an antacid and in soda-acid fire extinguishers.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Washing Soda</h3>
        <p>Sodium carbonate decahydrate (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O). Used in glass, soap and paper industries and for removing permanent hardness of water.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Plaster of Paris</h3>
        <p>Calcium sulphate hemihydrate (CaSO₄·½H₂O) obtained by heating gypsum at 373 K. Used for setting fractured bones and making casts.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Gypsum</h3>
        <p>Calcium sulphate dihydrate (CaSO₄·2H₂O). On heating at 373 K it forms Plaster of Paris.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Reactivity Series</h3>
        <p>A series of metals arranged in the order of their decreasing reactivity. K &gt; Na &gt; Ca &gt; Mg &gt; Al &gt; Zn &gt; Fe &gt; Pb &gt; H &gt; Cu &gt; Hg &gt; Ag &gt; Au.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Amphoteric Oxide</h3>
        <p>An oxide that can react with both acids and bases to form salt and water (e.g., Al₂O₃, ZnO).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Roasting</h3>
        <p>The process of heating a sulphide ore strongly in excess of air so that it is converted into its oxide.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Calcination</h3>
        <p>The process of heating a carbonate ore strongly in limited supply of air so that it is converted into its oxide.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Thermite Reaction</h3>
        <p>A highly exothermic reaction in which aluminium acts as a reducing agent and reduces iron(III) oxide to molten iron. Used for welding railway tracks.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Ionic Bond / Electrovalent Bond</h3>
        <p>The chemical bond formed by the complete transfer of electrons from a metal atom to a non-metal atom, resulting in the formation of oppositely charged ions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Covalent Bond</h3>
        <p>The chemical bond formed by the mutual sharing of electrons between two atoms (usually non-metals).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Catenation</h3>
        <p>The property of carbon atoms to link with other carbon atoms through covalent bonds to form long chains, branched chains or rings.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Tetravalency of Carbon</h3>
        <p>Carbon has four valence electrons and therefore forms four covalent bonds with other atoms.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Saturated Hydrocarbons</h3>
        <p>Hydrocarbons in which all carbon-carbon bonds are single bonds (alkanes). They undergo substitution reactions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Unsaturated Hydrocarbons</h3>
        <p>Hydrocarbons that contain at least one carbon-carbon double bond (alkenes) or triple bond (alkynes). They undergo addition reactions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Homologous Series</h3>
        <p>A series of organic compounds having the same functional group and similar chemical properties, in which each successive member differs by a –CH₂ group.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Functional Group</h3>
        <p>An atom or group of atoms that determines the characteristic chemical properties of an organic compound (e.g., –OH, –COOH, –CHO).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Addition Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which atoms or groups of atoms are added across a double or triple bond of an unsaturated compound.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Substitution Reaction</h3>
        <p>A reaction in which an atom or group of atoms in a molecule is replaced by another atom or group of atoms.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Esterification</h3>
        <p>The reaction between a carboxylic acid and an alcohol in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid to form an ester and water.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Saponification</h3>
        <p>The alkaline hydrolysis of an ester to form the sodium salt of carboxylic acid (soap) and alcohol.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Micelle</h3>
        <p>A spherical aggregate of soap molecules in water in which the hydrophobic tails are directed inwards and hydrophilic heads are directed outwards.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== CORE EXAM NOTES ==================== -->
  <section id="notes">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Core Exam Notes</h2>
      <p>High-yield points, shortcuts, common mistakes and board-focused rules. Read these before every test.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="def-grid">

      <!-- Reaction Type Identification -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>How to Identify Reaction Type Instantly</h3>
        <p>
          <b>Combination:</b> Many reactants → One product<br>
          <b>Decomposition:</b> One reactant → Many products<br>
          <b>Displacement:</b> A + BC → AC + B<br>
          <b>Double Displacement:</b> AB + CD → AD + CB<br>
          <b>Redox:</b> Oxidation and reduction occur together<br>
          <b>Neutralisation:</b> Acid + Base → Salt + Water
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Oxidation Reduction Shortcut -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Oxidation & Reduction Shortcut (Class 10 Level)</h3>
        <p>
          <b>Oxidation:</b> Gain of oxygen <b>or</b> Loss of hydrogen<br>
          <b>Reduction:</b> Loss of oxygen <b>or</b> Gain of hydrogen<br><br>
          When both happen in the same reaction → <b>Redox reaction</b>.<br>
          Example: CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O<br>
          CuO is reduced, H₂ is oxidised.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Colour Memory -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Must-Remember Colour Changes</h3>
        <p>
          CuSO₄ (blue) → FeSO₄ (pale green) + Cu (reddish-brown)<br>
          Cu → CuO (black) on heating<br>
          CuO (black) → Cu (reddish-brown) by H₂<br>
          FeSO₄ (green) → Fe₂O₃ (brown) on heating<br>
          Pb(NO₃)₂ → PbO (yellow) + NO₂ (brown gas)<br>
          AgCl (white) → Ag (grey) in sunlight<br>
          ZnO : yellow when hot, white when cold<br>
          PbI₂ : bright yellow precipitate
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Gas Tests -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Important Gas Tests</h3>
        <p>
          <b>H₂:</b> Burns with a pop sound<br>
          <b>O₂:</b> Relights a glowing splint<br>
          <b>CO₂:</b> Turns lime water milky<br>
          <b>Cl₂:</b> Greenish-yellow gas, bleaching action<br>
          <b>SO₂:</b> Colourless gas with burning sulphur smell<br>
          <b>NO₂:</b> Brown fumes
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- State Symbols -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>State Symbols & Arrows</h3>
        <p>
          (s) = solid &nbsp;&nbsp; (l) = liquid &nbsp;&nbsp; (g) = gas &nbsp;&nbsp; (aq) = aqueous solution<br>
          ↓ = precipitate &nbsp;&nbsp; ↑ = gas evolved<br>
          Always write state symbols in board answers when asked.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Conditions -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Conditions That Must Be Written</h3>
        <p>
          Heat, sunlight, electricity, catalyst (Ni, Pd), conc. H₂SO₄, 443 K, excess air, limited air, electrolysis of brine, etc.<br>
          Missing the condition often costs marks in equation questions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Reactivity Series -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Reactivity Series (Must Memorise)</h3>
        <p>
          K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Pb > (H) > Cu > Hg > Ag > Au<br><br>
          A metal can displace any metal that is below it from its salt solution.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Amphoteric Oxides -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Amphoteric Oxides</h3>
        <p>
          Al₂O₃ and ZnO react with both acids and bases.<br>
          This is a very common 2 or 3 mark question.<br>
          Always write both reactions when asked.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Roasting vs Calcination -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Roasting vs Calcination</h3>
        <p>
          <b>Roasting:</b> Sulphide ore + excess air → Oxide + SO₂<br>
          <b>Calcination:</b> Carbonate ore + limited air → Oxide + CO₂<br>
          Both are done before reduction of the metal oxide.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Thermite -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Thermite Reaction – Key Points</h3>
        <p>
          Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → 2Fe + Al₂O₃ + Heat<br>
          Aluminium acts as reducing agent.<br>
          Highly exothermic → molten iron produced.<br>
          Used for welding railway tracks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Corrosion Prevention -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Prevention of Corrosion / Rusting</h3>
        <p>
          1. Painting<br>
          2. Oiling / Greasing<br>
          3. Galvanisation (coating with zinc)<br>
          4. Chrome plating<br>
          5. Anodising<br>
          6. Making alloys (stainless steel)<br>
          7. Sacrificial protection
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Rancidity Prevention -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Prevention of Rancidity</h3>
        <p>
          1. Adding antioxidants<br>
          2. Refrigeration<br>
          3. Storing in airtight containers<br>
          4. Flushing with nitrogen gas<br>
          5. Keeping away from light
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- pH Important Values -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Important pH Values</h3>
        <p>
          Gastric juice → ~1.2 (highly acidic)<br>
          Lemon juice → ~2.2<br>
          Pure water / NaCl → 7 (neutral)<br>
          Blood → 7.4<br>
          Milk of magnesia → ~10<br>
          Sodium hydroxide → ~14
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Important Salts -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Important Salts – Formulae & Uses</h3>
        <p>
          Bleaching powder → CaOCl₂ → bleaching & disinfectant<br>
          Baking soda → NaHCO₃ → baking, antacid, fire extinguisher<br>
          Washing soda → Na₂CO₃·10H₂O → cleaning, glass, removing hardness<br>
          Plaster of Paris → CaSO₄·½H₂O → casts, statues<br>
          Gypsum → CaSO₄·2H₂O → source of POP
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Carbon Chapter Shortcuts -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Carbon Chapter – Quick Rules</h3>
        <p>
          Saturated → Substitution reaction<br>
          Unsaturated → Addition reaction<br>
          Alcohol + Carboxylic acid → Ester (esterification)<br>
          Ester + NaOH → Soap + Alcohol (saponification)<br>
          Ethanol + Na → Sodium ethoxide + H₂<br>
          Ethanol + conc. H₂SO₄ (443 K) → Ethene
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Common Board Mistakes -->
