        <h3>Common Mistakes That Cost Marks</h3>
        <p>
          • Forgetting to balance the equation<br>
          • Missing state symbols when asked<br>
          • Writing wrong condition (especially 443 K, sunlight, heat)<br>
          • Confusing roasting and calcination<br>
          • Writing CuSO₄ colour as green instead of blue<br>
          • Forgetting that ZnO is yellow when hot
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Activity Series Application -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>How to Predict Displacement</h3>
        <p>
          Look at the reactivity series.<br>
          Only a metal placed <b>above</b> another metal can displace it from its salt solution.<br>
          Example: Zn can displace Cu, Fe, Ag etc. but Cu cannot displace Zn.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Ionic vs Covalent -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Ionic vs Covalent Compounds (Quick Difference)</h3>
        <p>
          <b>Ionic:</b> Metal + Non-metal, electron transfer, high melting point, conduct electricity in molten/aqueous state<br>
          <b>Covalent:</b> Non-metal + Non-metal, electron sharing, low melting point, generally do not conduct electricity
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Why Carbon Forms Many Compounds -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Why Carbon Forms Large Number of Compounds</h3>
        <p>
          1. Catenation (self-linking ability)<br>
          2. Tetravalency<br>
          3. Ability to form single, double and triple bonds<br>
          4. Ability to form straight chains, branched chains and rings
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Soap vs Detergent -->
      <div class="def-card">
        <h3>Soap vs Detergent (Exam Point)</h3>
        <p>
          Soaps form scum with hard water.<br>
          Detergents work well even in hard water because they do not form insoluble scum with calcium and magnesium ions.
        </p>
